<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    IPC contracts (System.Text.Json source-generated contexts, docs/07_IPC.md)
    and the C# mirrors of the native shared-memory structs.

    The mirrors are the dangerous part: FlFrameRecord, FlShmHandshake,
    FlWriterState and FlControlBlock each exist twice, once in C++
    (src/native/FrameLedger.Shm/include/fl_shm.h, which is NORMATIVE) and once
    here as [StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential)]. ShmLayoutMirrorTests asserts
    size AND every field offset on both sides against the JSON emitted by
    tools/fl-layout-dump, and asserts that every mirror is BLITTABLE — offsets
    alone cannot see the difference between a fixed buffer and a marshalled
    string, and only one of those can be read out of a memory-mapped view.

    Struct drift between the two layers is the most dangerous silent bug in this
    architecture: nothing crashes, the Agent just reads garbage into fields that
    look plausible (CLAUDE.md §Struct mirroring, docs/12_BUILD.md §Managed build).

    The IPC contracts half is still unwritten; only the mirrors are here.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      For `fixed byte BuildId[32]` and `fixed uint Reserved[N]`. Those are the
      native char[32] / uint32[N] arrays, and a fixed buffer is the only C#
      construct that reproduces them WITHOUT making the struct non-blittable.
      [MarshalAs(ByValTStr)] would satisfy every offset assertion and then fail
      to read out of a MemoryMappedViewAccessor, which is the entire purpose.
    -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>

    <!--
      FOUR ANALYZER RULES ARE SUPPRESSED FOR THIS PROJECT ONLY, and each is
      suppressed because obeying it would BREAK THE MIRROR. That is the whole
      justification: these types are not a C# API, they are a byte-for-byte
      restatement of fl_shm.h, and every one of these rules is advice about
      idiomatic C# API design.

        CA1028  "make the underlying type Int32". The native enums are uint32_t
                (FlStatus) and uint8_t (FlApi, FlUpscaler, FlFgMode, FlRtFlags,
                FlMeasured). Widening any of them changes the size of the field
                that carries it and the mirror test fails, which is the test
                doing its job against a change this rule would have caused.
        CA1711  "do not end a type name in 'Flags'". FlRtFlags IS the native
                name. Renaming it makes the two sides harder to diff by eye,
                which is how a drifting field gets missed in review.
        CA1716  the namespace FrameLedger.Shared collides with a VB keyword.
                It is the namespace CLAUDE.md §Solution layout assigns, it
                predates this file, and renaming it is a solution-wide change
                for a language this project does not target.
        MA0048  one type per file. This file mirrors ONE header, and the four
                structs plus their enums are a single contract that has to be
                read together — the region offsets only make sense beside the
                structs they bound. Splitting a 64-byte ABI across ten files
                makes the thing this project exists to keep consistent harder
                to check, and the mirror test is what enforces it either way.
        CA1051  "do not declare visible instance fields". Public fields are the
                point: Marshal.OffsetOf and the mirror test address them by
                name, and properties have no defined offset at all. A property
                cannot mirror a struct member.
        CA1815  "override Equals and ==". These are windows onto shared memory,
                not values anyone compares. Two records with identical bytes are
                two different frames, so a meaningful Equals does not exist —
                and writing one over a `fixed byte[32]` would be ceremony that
                obscures the layout this file exists to state plainly.

      Everything else stays on: this is a targeted list, not AnalysisLevel
      turned down.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1028;CA1711;CA1716;MA0048;CA1051;CA1815</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
